    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": 15,
      "title": "The 7-day artifact limit, and what the first rollup covers",
      "description": "Why a per-test trend starts when it starts.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 6,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 39
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**A run's per-test result lives only in its GitHub artifact, and GitHub deletes that after 7 days.** So the rollup can only record a day while that day's runs still have artifacts. The first rollup covers the runs of the last 7 days and nothing older: history before that cannot be reconstructed, and the coverage panel above shows it as a day that was never rolled up, not as a green day.\n\nRun `ci_observe_trends.py --days 7` daily to keep the rollup complete. Missing a day leaves a permanent hole in per-test history; the job-level numbers on the Overview section are unaffected.\n\nA run that uploaded no artifact, an artifact larger than the download bound, and an artifact with no JUnit file all read as **no test data** for that run. They are counted in `runs_unavailable`, and they never contribute a pass."
      }
    }
